"Our faith should lead us to understand diversity as a gift from God, equity as a moral imperative,
and inclusion and belonging as fruits of the Spirit." - Candler School of Theology, Emory University
Diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) has been increasing in prevalence particularly since 2020,
with DEI policies and initiatives being increasingly implemented in workplaces, policies, schools,
and governments. These concepts of diversity, equity and inclusion resound with Christian beliefs.
Christians are called to uphold them when interacting with each other and the world, according to
the example of Jesus. However, as these concepts are often defined and interpreted according to
a secular viewpoint, there is tension for Christians in the way DEI is implemented and in the way it
contradicts with teachings of doctrine, thus leading to disputes. In affirming this thesis, this essay
will focus on a secular interpretation of DEI and how it interacts with Christianity.
Christianity and DEI in its current secular interpretation are incompatible. This is because DEI is
not simply an institutional act against racism, but a completely different moral system, whose
foundations are different to those of Christianity, leading to intuitive contradictions between DEI
and Christian morality. These foundations are based on the distinctions between secular and
religious worldviews. While secular society is based on cultural relativism, arguing for individual
truth that varies between individuals, Christianity is based on absolutism, positing that truth is
absolute and given by God (2 Timothy 3:16 - "All scripture is God-breathed"). Secular societies
also believe that each person is fundamentally good, we deserve good things, and that the
purpose of humanity is life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness. The Christian worldview, however,
states that each person is inherently sinful due to original sin (Romans 6:23 - "For all have sinned
and fall short of the glory of God") and therefore deserve judgment from God, and the purpose of
humanity is to glorify God, as seen in 1 Corinthians: "So whether you eat or drink, or whatever you
do, do all for the glory of God." These fundamental differences in worldviews lead to different
interpretations of such concepts as diversity, equity and inclusion, which this essay will explore in
each corresponding section. Differences in language use of these concepts further magnify the
differences between the viewpoints as each side uses the concepts through their own ideological
lens, thus leading to disputes.

DIVERSITY
Diversity is defined in the secular interpretation as a variety of individual differences that exist in a
community, such as gender, race, ethnicity, class, age, disability, etc. Diversity in this sense can be
complementary to Christian beliefs, as it deconstructs ethnocentrism, clannishness, and narrow
mindsets that are too often prevalent in society. Each person having individual differences aligns
with the Christian worldview, however the focus in the Christian perspective of diversity is the
variety of spiritual, God-given gifts and abilities that is bestowed upon his people to reflect the
breadth of his qualities and character. Romans 12:6 says "We have different gifts, according to the
grace that is given to each of us". This is reinforced in 1 Corinthians 12, where Paul speaks about
the different spiritual gifts that the church has, and argues that if everyone had the same gift, the
church (wider society in a secular perspective) would be impoverished due to a lack of a variety of
abilities.
The biblical sense of diversity also reflects ethnic diversity as well as a uniqueness of spiritual gifts.
This is seen in Jesus' teaching and God's plan for humanity. Revelations 7:9 says, "there before
me was a great multitude, too many to count, from every nation, tribe, people, and language." This
is to be achieved through the Great Commission in Matthew 28:19 - "Therefore go and make
disciples of all nations, baptising them in the name of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit." Scripture
shapes how Christianity sees ethnic diversity - it is valuable and cherished in God's kingdom.
However, even within Christianity itself, there are disputes surrounding diversity. Often in modern
churches, diversity is made the highest priority. In a Lifeway survey of 1000 Protestant pastors,
88% agreed that churches should strive for racial diversity, citing Revelation 5:9 and 7:9 as
reasons. Pastor and missiologist Ed Stetzer says, "The beauty of heaven is seen in the diversity of
the church. Multiethnic churches are a preview of God's eternal kingdom." However, others argue
that it is unreasonable to expect each individual church to have ethnic diversity. Each church will
most likely reflect the ethnic diversity that is already present in the geographical area. However,
according to Stetzer, such churches are seen as providing an imperfect view of heaven due to
their lack of ethnic diversity. Contrary to Stetzer's thinking, some argue that passages like
Revelations 7:9 are not prescriptive, but descriptive. According to Christian doctrine, diversity
should not be the goal - bringing glory to God should be the goal; diversity is merely a byproduct of
the gospel and the Great Commission. A focus on creating diversity undermines the fundamental
doctrinal beliefs of Christianity, which is seen in the next verse in Revelation 7:10 - the great
multitude was "crying out in a loud voice", worshipping God. As Shai Linne puts it in his book The
New Reformation, "Ethnic diversity is not virtuous in and of itself... hell is also a very diverse
place."
Furthermore, secular interpretations of diversity often call for a diversity of perspectives. While this
is beneficial in some senses and can be allowed in Christianity within non-doctrinal issues or non-
essential doctrine, a diversity of perspectives cannot exist within matters of doctrine and dogma,
which must be adhered to in order for the validity of the religious tradition to stand. In Ephesians
4:1-7, God calls for unity of the church. Amos 3:3 says "Can two walk together, unless they are
agreed?" However, secular diversity is often extrapolated to include the acceptance of cultures that
are antithetical to fundamental biblical doctrine. Evidently, this leads to a dispute between secular
interpretations of diversity and Christian beliefs. In Translations of the Wokish, it is said that
"diversity in the critical social justice usage refers to a uniformity of viewpoint in ideological issues."
This sense of diversity in perspectives argues for ideological conformity instead of ideological
unity. For religious traditions to uphold their doctrinal beliefs, ideological conformity to secular
perspectives cannot occur.

EQUITY
Equity is secularly defined as "levelling the playing field" - providing equal outcomes via
personalised approaches to individual needs. As Kamala Harris said, "Equity means we all end up
in the same place." This approach of equity argues that equal outcomes must be attained; if the
outcomes are unequal, the system is seen as unacceptable. A distinction must be made here
between equity and equality. Equity is often contrasted with formal equality, which includes laws
and policies designed to ensure everyone is treated in the exact same way. Equality argues for
equal opportunities rather than equal outcomes. However, as seen in the treatment of African-
American people in America post-Civil War, when slavery had been abolished, they had, in theory,
all the rights and privileges of white citizens, but in reality most ended up as sharecroppers whose
lives were only marginally better than before. Though formal equality and equal opportunities
existed, equal outcomes were not reached. Hence, it is argued according to this view that equity
must be implemented instead - where unequal treatments are applied deliberately, in the name of
justice.
However, DEI policies have led to selection based on race/identity markers rather than on
qualifications and merits, in an effort to provide equal outcomes, which Christianity argues against,
as this secular form of equity leads to a form of discrimination that Scripture condemns. 1 Samuel
16:7 says God looks at the heart instead of the outward appearance, like any external identity
markers. In Exodus 16:28, Moses is commanded to appoint leaders that are capable and fear
God, who are trustworthy and hate dishonest gain. Meritocracy is used as a basis for selecting
leaders in Scripture, and in the Christian perspective, should also be used in the modern day for
hiring employees. According to the secular view of equity, some activists openly argue for and
promote discrimination. African-American activist Ibram X. Kendi said, "The only remedy to past
discrimination is present discrimination. The only remedy to present discrimination is future
discrimination." The focus in implementing equity in the secular worldview is for humans to take
control of justice by creating unjust policies, which Christianity argues against.
Aside from this dispute on equity, scripture aligns with both equity and equality in various
passages. Equality in the biblical view is based on a belief that all people are made in God's image
(Genesis 1:27). This is supported by Galatians 3:28, which says "There is no longer Jew or Greek,
there is no longer slave or free, there is no longer male or female; for you are all one in Christ
Jesus." This passage argues against superiority due to inherent categorical differences. Rather
than eradicating the differences that make each person unique, Christianity rejects attaching
hierarchal value to these distinctions, thus maintaining that all people have an equal inherent worth
and value. Due to this, partiality and favouritism is condemned in Scripture. In the Old Testament,
Israelites were forbidden from showing partiality to the rich or the poor. In the New Testament,
James 2:1 says, "My brothers and sisters, believers... must not show favouritism." James 2:9 says
"if you show favouritism, you sin." Equity is also seen in the Bible. In Acts 4:35, the money was
"distributed to anyone who had need." In Mosaic law in Leviticus 12:6-8, provisions were made for
those experiencing poverty. These passages model equity and highlight the distribution of
resources to support certain individuals as necessary.

INCLUSION
The secular interpretation of inclusion is to provide space for each individual to participate,
regardless of differences. Inclusion can be defined as the opposite of oppression - it is the
liberation from oppression to participate. It follows, then, that the different interpretations of
oppression in the two worldviews lead to different definitions of inclusion. While Christianity defines
oppression as an unjust, tyrannical rule, including violence, coercion, and tyranny, the secular
worldview defines oppression as hegemonic norms that exist within society which "manifest in
racism, sexism, heterosexism, religious oppression, classism, ableism, ageism/adultism...
eradicating oppression ultimately requires struggle against all its forms", according to Adams et al,
in Teaching for Diversity and Social Justice. This grouping of all the "forms" under an umbrella
term of oppression requires one to oppose heterosexism and cisgenderism as well as racism and
sexism to be truly inclusive. As Ibram X. Kendi writes in his book How to be an Antiracist, "One
cannot be antiracist if they are homophobic or transphobic." However, this clashes with
fundamental doctrinal beliefs in Christianity, which does not traditionally permit homosexuality or
transgenderism.
Furthermore, the secular interpretation of inclusion requires affirmation. The prevailing view in
society today is that affirmation equates love, and the opposite equates hate, particularly in areas
of gender and sexual identity. In the example of LGBTQ communities, inclusion according to
secular worldviews requires one to accept the man-made identity of individuals within the
community, which clashes with the traditional Christian view of individuals instead having a God-
given identity, being made in God's image (Genesis 1:27). According to Scripture, God commands
his followers to call out what Christianity views as sin. As Baptist minister Dr Martin Luther King Jr
said, "We are called to be people of conviction, not conformity; of moral nobility, not solid
respectability." Perhaps an example of how this can be resolved while still showing inclusion
towards such individuals can be seen in the Catholic church's approach to same-sex marriages -
they recognise the legality and status of the marriage but cannot sanctify it according to doctrine.
While the secular perspective of inclusion includes identifying with certain social groups and
incorporating them into society, the Christian perspective of inclusion focuses on reaching out to
and including all of humanity in God's kingdom, thus transcending social boundaries and divisions.
In Jesus' ministry on earth, he reached the poor, sick, and marginalised - prostitutes, tax collectors,
lepers - calling them to become holy, pure and obedient. In Matthew 16:24, Jesus says "If anyone
would come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me." Furthermore, in
John 8, when dealing with the adulterous woman, Jesus does not condemn her for her sin but at
the same time does not excuse it, instead calling her to "go and sin no more" (John 8:11). This
highlights the key differences between secular and Christian worldviews - while the secular
perspective argues for the acceptance of people 'just as they are' due to truth being relative and
individualistic, the absolutist Christian perspective that Jesus demonstrates argues instead for
transformation to be 'just like he is'.
The Christian viewpoint of inclusion further maintains that inclusion is achieved through the
recognition of doctrine in that all people are unified in their sin, and in their redemption in
Christ. Shai Linne says in The New Reformation that "the key to addressing ethnic disunity is the
proper application of doctrine of justification by faith alone" (sola fida). Ethnic diversity is ultimately
eradicated in the view that all people, regardless of ethnicity, have sinned, and all people are
justified through Jesus' death on the cross. Shai Linne goes on to state that "unity is living out the
oneness that is already ours in Christ." Instead of creating inclusion, according to the Christian
viewpoint, inclusion is already achieved by the participation of people in God's kingdom. Galatians
3:28 says "There is no longer Jew or Greek, there is no longer slave or free, there is no longer
male or female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus." While the secular perspective of inclusion
focuses on individual participation, with an emphasis on the self, the Christian perspective
maintains that unity and interdependence are necessary for inclusion. In his letters, Paul exhorts
the Church to uphold unity sacrificially. Philippians 2:4 says "Do not look only at your own
interests, but the interests of others, too." This principle goes in both directions - the majority
should sacrifice their preferences for that of the minority, but the minority should also do the same.
This leads on to the way in which Christian traditions can respond to and approach DEI, posited by
evangelical sociologist George Yancey in his book Beyond Racial Division. He outlines a model for
dialogue around DEI, which he terms "collaborative conversations", and splits it up into 3 parts:

1. Active listening: Yancey's model for active listening involves listening to the other point of view,
then reframing the other perspective in one's own words to show understanding. He maintains that
individuals do not have to agree on everything to have frutiful conversations about DEI. Rather,
each side should aim to listen to the other perspective to facilitate greater understanding.
2. Mutual responsibility: institutional culture is based on the majority culture, therefore it follows
that the majority will have to make the most adjustments to foster diversity, equity, and inclusion,
but this does not mean they should just "shut up and listen", rather they should also provide input
and offer solutions.
3. Practical focus: Yancey argues that implementing diversity, equity and inclusion should focus on
achievable approaches and outcomes which all can agree on. Within the Church, a scripture-
based approach should be used rather than a political consensus, in order to adhere to doctrinal
beliefs. Within this, it is important according to Christianity to "do everything in love" according to 1
Corinthians 8, remembering the second greatest commandment when advocating for the inclusion
and equity of diverse people: to "love thy neighbour as thyself".
Therefore, it has been explored through this essay how disputes arise when secular interpretations
of diversity, equity and inclusion intersect with Christian perspectives, due to the fundamental
differences in worldviews that surface when each side defines and interprets diversity, equity and
inclusion according to their understandings and draws conclusions based on such. Perhaps if
George Yancey's model of dialogue is used as a guideline, or another similar to it, each side may
be able to better understand the other and lead to a more diverse, equitable, and inclusive
world. After all, diversity, equity and inclusion are concepts that are universally advocated for -
simply in different ways.
